
########## frequency.py ##########

==============================================================================
1. NEAREST EQUAL-TEMPERED PITCH TO EACH PUBLISHED FREQUENCY
==============================================================================
               published    nearest 12-TET       exact       error     cents
     246.910 Hz      Sec. 2.3, reactor  B3                  246.942      -0.032     -0.22
      61.720 Hz          Sec. 3, field  B1                   61.735      -0.015     -0.43

  A cent is 1/100 of a semitone; the just-noticeable pitch difference for
  a trained listener is about 5 cents.  Both published frequencies are
  inside that, i.e. they are the note B as a tuner would set it.
==============================================================================
2. THE OPTICAL TIMING IS THE SAME NUMBER
==============================================================================
  pulse                     50.0 us
  dark phase              4000.0 us
  period                   4.050 ms
  repetition rate         246.91 Hz
  cymatic frequency       246.91 Hz
  difference            3.58e-03 Hz   (1.5e-05 relative)

  Sec. 2.2 derives the optical timing from 'the enzymatic turnover rate of
  the Calvin cycle'.  Sec. 2.3 derives the acoustic frequency from guard
  cell mechanics.  Measured, they agree to 1.5 parts in 100 000, which is
  the rounding of 246.91 Hz to two decimals -- they are one number, and
  that number is B3.
==============================================================================
3. THE SUBHARMONIC
==============================================================================
  F_reactor / F_field = 4.00049
  f/4 is the 4th subharmonic, two octaves down -- the naming is correct.
  wavelength at 61.72 Hz:  5.56 m
  wavelength at 246.91 Hz: 1.389 m
==============================================================================
4. THE BAND
==============================================================================
  band asserted by the preprint, Sec. 3:           50.0 -   120.0 Hz
  band of the review it cites (Hassanien 2014):   100.0 -  1000.0 Hz
    that review's working level: 70 dB SPL at 30-60 m

           frequency    in preprint band    in cited band
   reactor 246.91 Hz               False             True
   field    61.72 Hz                True            False

  Sec. 3 divides by four so that the result 'falls perfectly within the
  empirically proven PAFT sensitive band'.  Measured against the band of
  the review it cites for that claim, the division does the opposite:
  246.91 Hz was inside, 61.72 Hz is below it.

########## light.py ##########

==============================================================================
1. THE DUTY CYCLE AND THE AVERAGE PHOTON FLUX
==============================================================================
  duty cycle                     1.2346 %      (1 : 81.0)
  peak irradiance                4500.0 W/m^2  =     20700 umol/m^2/s =  10.35 x full sun
  time-averaged irradiance         55.6 W/m^2  =       256 umol/m^2/s =   0.13 x full sun

  If the 4500 W/m^2 of Sec. 2.2 is total rather than PAR irradiance, the
  photon flux is lower still; this takes the figure at its most favourable.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Carbon fixed over a day is set by the average flux, not the peak: the
  pulse is 50 us and the Calvin cycle integrates over seconds.
  C3 light saturation: 400 (lettuce) ... 1400 (wheat) umol/m^2/s
  the average produced here is 1.57 x BELOW the lowest
  of those and 5.48 x below the highest.

  NPQ is a saturation-regime loss.  At 256 umol/m^2/s a continuous-light
  control is not light-saturated either, so it engages little NPQ: the
  mechanism the design exists to bypass is not active in the regime the
  design creates.  The comparison in Sec. 1 is against continuous
  HIGH-intensity light, which this scheme does not deliver on average.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Ceiling on the gain even where NPQ is fully engaged: the preprint puts
  the loss at up to 60 %, so recovering all of it is 1/(1-0.6) = 2.50 x.
  Bounded, and bounded by a number the preprint supplies itself.
==============================================================================
2. WHICH TIMESCALE THE 4 ms DARK PHASE MATCHES
==============================================================================
  dark phase, Sec. 2.2                          4.00 ms
  RuBisCO turnover 1/kcat, kcat = 2-4 /s      250 - 500 ms   <- 'the Calvin cycle'
  plastoquinol oxidation at cyt b6f              2 -  20 ms   <- the PQ pool
  ratio of the Calvin figure to 4 ms:           62 - 125 x off

  4 ms is the right order for reoxidation of the plastoquinone pool, which
  is exactly what Sec. 1 says the design must relieve.  The timing works.
  The justification given for it names the Calvin cycle, which is two
  orders of magnitude slower.  Right number, wrong mechanism.
==============================================================================
3. GRAMS PER KILOWATT-HOUR IS INVARIANT UNDER PULSING
==============================================================================
  scheme                                               g/m^2/d    W/m^2    g/kWh
  pulsed, 1.23 % duty, NPQ fully bypassed                 33.1    111.1    12.42
  continuous at the same average flux, no NPQ             33.1    111.1    12.42
  the same, if NPQ cost the published 60 % here           13.2    111.1     4.97
  continuous at full sun, NPQ at the published 60 %      103.7    869.6     4.97

  Rows 1 and 2 are identical because pulsing divides the dry matter and
  the electrical input by the same duty cycle.  The ratio cannot move.
  Row 3 is hypothetical: at 256 umol/m^2/s there is little NPQ to pay.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The invariance is structural, so it must survive varying the duty cycle
  while the peak stays at 4500 W/m^2.  Rows past light saturation are
  marked (*): there the linear quantum-yield model no longer holds, and
  the real g/kWh would FALL, never rise.
    duty %   avg umol/m^2/s    g/m^2/d     W/m^2     g/kWh
     1.235              256       33.1     111.1     12.42
     5.000             1035      134.1     450.0     12.42
    10.000             2070      268.3     900.0     12.42 (*)
    25.000             5175      670.7    2250.0     12.42 (*)
   100.000            20700     2682.7    9000.0     12.42 (*)
  spread of g/kWh across an 81-fold range of duty cycle: 3.553e-15  (floating point only)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  g/kWh does depend on the quantum yield, and on nothing else in the
  design.  Doubling the assumed yield doubles it exactly:
   quantum yield     g/kWh    ratio to the first row
           0.020      4.97                    1.0000
           0.040      9.94                    2.0000
           0.050     12.42                    2.5000
           0.080     19.87                    4.0000
           0.125     31.05                    6.2500

  So the only lever on energy efficiency is the quantum yield, and the
  most that bypassing NPQ can add to it is the 2.50 x ceiling of Sec. 1
  above -- in a regime where, as measured, NPQ is barely engaged.
  Sec. 5 claims the reactor 'redefines the gram-per-watt limit of CEA'.
  Commercial vertical farming already sits at roughly 5-10 g dry per kWh.
==============================================================================
4. THE 100 % PHOTON CAPTURE CLAIM
==============================================================================
  Sec. 2.1: planar arrays lose 40-50 %, the cylinder lets
  'photon capture efficiency approach the theoretical maximum of 100%'.
  PAR absorptance of a single green leaf: 0.84 - 0.90
  so a photon striking one leaf once is absorbed with probability <= 0.90.
  A closed reflective cavity can exceed that by multiple passes, so 100 %
  is not excluded outright -- but the bound is then set by the reflectance
  of the enclosure wall, which is where the LEDs are.  This is recorded as
  an unquantified claim rather than as a defect.

########## acoustics.py ##########

==============================================================================
1. BLADE-PASS FREQUENCY AGAINST THE TARGET
==============================================================================
   rotor rpm   blade-pass Hz  harmonic of BPF at 61.72 Hz
         9.0           0.450                         137.2
        14.0           0.700                          88.2
        18.0           0.900                          68.6

  rotor speed for which BPF = 61.72 Hz:      1234.4 rpm
  rated rotor speed of the class:               14.0 rpm   (88 x)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   blade R     tip speed    Mach     tip accel        in g
        40       5171 m/s    15.1     668389 m/s2       68133
        50       6463 m/s    18.8     835486 m/s2       85167
        60       7756 m/s    22.6    1002583 m/s2      102200

  'RPM Lock: Electromagnetically braking the rotor via the grid inverter
  forces the turbine to maintain exact rotational speeds ... ensuring a
  phase-stable 61.72 Hz emission' requires 1234 rpm at the
  rotor.  Braking cannot raise a rotor speed, and the speed asked for is
  supersonic at the blade tip.

  Taken instead as a harmonic of the blade pass, 61.72 Hz is near the 88th.
  'Pitch Tuning' shifting noise by 'a fraction of a degree' moves broadband
  stall noise, which has no line structure to place at a chosen frequency.
==============================================================================
2. THE LEVEL THAT REACHES THE FIELD
==============================================================================
  PAFT in the cited review works at 70 dB SPL (+/- 5) at 30-60 m.
  Declared apparent sound power of a 2-3 MW turbine: 100-110 dB re 1 pW (31.6 mW at 105 dB), broadband.

    r [m]   turbine | Lw needed for 65     70  75 dB |  short at 70     ratio
      100   57.0 dB |            113.0  118.0  123.0 |      13.0 dB       20 x
      300   47.5 dB |            122.5  127.5  132.5 |      22.5 dB      179 x
      500   43.0 dB |            127.0  132.0  137.0 |      27.0 dB      497 x
     1000   37.0 dB |            133.0  138.0  143.0 |      33.0 dB     1987 x
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Sensitivity to the declared sound power, shortfall at 300 m and 70 dB:
    turbine Lw   shortfall   power ratio
        100 dB     27.5 dB           565 x
        105 dB     22.5 dB           179 x
        110 dB     17.5 dB            57 x

  Atmospheric absorption at 63 Hz is about 0.1 dB/km (ISO 9613-1), so over
  1 km it costs 0.1 dB against the 24 dB of geometric spreading from 60 m
  to 1 km.  Sec. 3 is right that a low frequency travels with 'minimal
  atmospheric attenuation' -- and that is not the loss that matters.
  Choosing the frequency addresses the negligible term.

  All of the above compares the turbine's whole broadband output against
  a requirement in one narrow band, so the real shortfall is larger.
==============================================================================
3. A WIND FARM AS A PHASED ARRAY
==============================================================================
  wavelength at 61.72 Hz:                     5.56 m
  element spacing for a grating-lobe-free array (lambda/2):   2.78 m
  actual spacing in a wind farm, 5-10 rotor diameters:
       5 D =     500 m =     90.0 wavelengths  (180 x the limit)
      10 D =    1000 m =    179.9 wavelengths  (360 x the limit)

  To hold a phase error below lambda/10, the timing of the blade pass
  must be stable to 1.62 ms.  One revolution at 14 rpm takes 4.29 s,
  so that is 0.136 degrees of rotor angle, held against gusts,
  across every turbine in the farm simultaneously.
==============================================================================
4. THE TRANSFORMER
==============================================================================
  line frequency                        50.00 Hz  (+/- 0.05 Hz, ENTSO-E)
  Magnetostrictive strain follows |B|, so the core is excited at 2f:
    100, 200, 300, 400 Hz
  A DC bias saturates alternate half cycles, which adds the odd lines:
    50, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300 Hz
  target                                61.72 Hz  = 1.2344 x f_line
  nearest line available                50.00 Hz  (off by 11.72 Hz)

  Every line a grid-locked core can emit is an integer multiple of 50 Hz.
  61.72 Hz is not one, and no firmware setting creates a spectral line
  at a frequency the excitation does not contain.  Producing it needs an
  independent source at that frequency -- which is a loudspeaker, not a
  'smart-grid inverter modulation'.

  Separately, 'asymmetric DC injection' is half-cycle saturation: the
  geomagnetically-induced-current failure mode, which raises magnetizing
  current, losses, hot-spot temperature and noise.  It is a damage
  mechanism, and grid codes limit injected DC to around 1 A for exactly
  that reason.

########## water.py ##########

==============================================================================
1. TRANSPIRATION ACROSS THE MIDDAY DEPRESSION
==============================================================================
  state                                  g_s     VPD            E
  morning, stomata open                 0.30     1.0     2.96 mmol/m^2/s
  midday, natural depression            0.10     3.0     2.96 mmol/m^2/s
  midday, held open as proposed         0.30     3.0     8.88 mmol/m^2/s

  The depression almost exactly cancels the rise in VPD: morning 2.96
  against natural midday 2.96 mmol/m^2/s.  That is what the response is for.
  Suppressing it multiplies midday transpiration by 3.0 x, at the hour of peak demand.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Sensitivity: the factor is g_s(held) / g_s(depressed) and does not depend
  on VPD at all, so it survives any choice of the midday VPD:
    VPD kPa         natural       held open    factor
        2.0       1.97 mmol       5.92 mmol       3.0 x
        2.5       2.47 mmol       7.40 mmol       3.0 x
        3.0       2.96 mmol       8.88 mmol       3.0 x
        4.0       3.95 mmol      11.85 mmol       3.0 x
        5.0       4.94 mmol      14.81 mmol       3.0 x
==============================================================================
2. WHAT IS BOUGHT WITH IT
==============================================================================
  Assimilation does rise when stomata reopen, but it saturates in CO2
  while transpiration stays linear in g_s, so water-use efficiency falls.
  A(g_s) = g_s / (g_s + K) with K = 0.10 as an illustrative shape;
  the value of K is chosen, not measured.
     g_s   A (relative)     E mmol   A/E (relative)
    0.10          0.500       2.96           16.883
    0.15          0.600       4.44           13.507
    0.20          0.667       5.92           11.256
    0.25          0.714       7.40            9.648
    0.30          0.750       8.88            8.442

  Tripling g_s from 0.10 to 0.30 multiplies
  transpiration by 3.0 x regardless of K, and assimilation by:
         K    A gain    water per gram of carbon
      0.03      1.18 x                      2.54 x
      0.05      1.29 x                      2.33 x
      0.10      1.50 x                      2.00 x
      0.20      1.80 x                      1.67 x
      0.50      2.25 x                      1.33 x
    linear      3.00 x                      1.00 x
  Only an exactly linear A(g_s) breaks even; every saturating shape costs
  more water per unit carbon, which is the definition of the trade-off
  the stomatal response exists to manage.
  The preprint states the direction of this itself: 'evapotranspiration
  rates will rise ... must be paired with continuous soil moisture
  monitoring and sufficient irrigation buffers'.  That is correct, and it
  is the cost rather than a caveat: the proposal is to defeat a drought
  response in open fields of winter wheat, sugar beet and potatoes.
==============================================================================
3. THE YIELD CLAIM
==============================================================================
  claimed net biomass increase: 15-18 %
  No derivation is given, and nothing in the paper links it to the
  61.72 Hz stimulation, to a dose, to a duration or to a crop.
  The cited PAFT review reports yield gains of 5.7 % (rice) to 37.1 %
  (cucumber) at 70 dB and 30-60 m from a dedicated generator, so the
  claimed range is plausible for that exposure.  What is not available is
  that exposure: see acoustics.py.
